BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
45 780 Td
(   - Stage 1: High Stationary \(Pre-Industrial Society\):) Tj
T*
(   * Crude Birth Rate \(CBR\): Very high \(35 to 45 per 1,000\), driven by high infant mortality,) Tj
T*
(   cultural norms, and agrarian labor needs.) Tj
T*
(   * Crude Death Rate \(CDR\): Very high and erratic \(30 to 45 per 1,000\), driven by recurrent) Tj
T*
(   famines, infectious epidemics \(cholera, plague\), and primitive sanitation.) Tj
T*
(   * Population Growth: Very low, near-zero long-term growth with wild cyclical fluctuations.) Tj
T*
(   - Stage 2: Early Expanding \(Developing Society\):) Tj
T*
(   * CDR collapses rapidly \(falling to 15-20 per 1,000\) due to clean drinking water, municipal) Tj
T*
(   sewage systems, antibiotics, vaccination, and food transport.) Tj
T*
(   * CBR remains stubbornly high due to cultural inertia and lack of family planning.) Tj
T*
(   * Population Dynamics: Rapid, explosive population growth! \(The historical phase responsible) Tj
T*
(   for global population surges\).) Tj
T*
(   - Stage 3: Late Expanding \(Maturing Developing Society\):) Tj
T*
(   * CBR begins to decline significantly \(dropping to 15-20 per 1,000\) driven by urbanization,) Tj
T*
(   female literacy, entry of women into the formal workforce, increased cost of child-rearing, and) Tj
T*
(   widespread contraception.) Tj
T*
(   * CDR continues to decline slowly \(stabilizing at 8-10 per 1,000\).) Tj
T*
(   * Population Growth: Continues to grow, but the rate of expansion decelerates.) Tj
T*
(   - Stage 4: Low Stationary \(Developed Industrial Society\):) Tj
T*
(   * Both CBR and CDR stabilize at low levels \(10 to 12 per 1,000\).) Tj
T*
(   * Total Fertility Rate \(TFR\) hovers near replacement level \(~2.1\). Population stabilizes into a) Tj
T*
(   stationary equilibrium.) Tj
T*
(   - Stage 5: Natural Decline \(Post-Industrial Society, e.g., Japan, Italy, Germany\):) Tj
T*
(   * CBR falls well below CDR \(TFR drops to 1.2 to 1.5\). Death rates rise slightly due to an aging) Tj
T*
(   demographic structure.) Tj
T*
(   * Population experiences persistent, natural contraction.) Tj
T*
(   3. The Demographic Dividend:) Tj
T*
(   - A strategic economic window occurring during Stages 2 and 3 when the birth rate decline) Tj
T*
(   produces a bulge in the working-age population \(15 to 64 years\) while the Dependency Ratio) Tj
T*
(   \(proportion of dependent children and elderly\) falls to an all-time low.) Tj
T*
(   - If accompanied by investments in education, healthcare, and employment creation, this) Tj
T*
(   demographic structure unleashes rapid GDP growth and national capital accumulation.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
