BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Examine the geometric morphology of Population Age Pyramids, contrasting Expansive, Constrictive, and Stationary profiles and their societal impacts.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Morphological Classification of Population Pyramids and Socio-Economic Implications:) Tj
T*
(   1. Structural Geometry of Age-Sex Pyramids:) Tj
T*
(   A population pyramid is a dual horizontal bar graph displaying the percentage distribution of a) Tj
T*
(   population across successive 5-year age cohorts \(from 0-4 up to 85+ years\), with males plotted) Tj
T*
(   to the left of a central axis and females to the right:) Tj
T*
(   2. The Three Primary Morphological Typologies:) Tj
T*
(   - 1. Expansive Population Pyramid \(Triangular Classic Shape\):) Tj
T*
(   * Geometric Profile: Wide, broad base tapering sharply to a pointed apex.) Tj
T*
(   * Demographic Characteristics: Very high proportion of children and youth \(< 15 years,) Tj
T*
(   comprising 35-45% of total population\) with high birth rates and rapid natural increase. Rapid) Tj
T*
(   narrowing reflects higher mortality across older age cohorts.) Tj
T*
(   * Typical Nations: Sub-Saharan Africa \(e.g., Niger, Uganda, Chad\).) Tj
T*
(   * Socio-Economic Challenges: High youth dependency burden, extreme pressure on primary school) Tj
T*
(   education, pediatric healthcare infrastructure, and future job creation demands.) Tj
T*
(   - 2. Constrictive Population Pyramid \(Beehive / Urn Shape\):) Tj
T*
(   * Geometric Profile: Narrow base at bottom, bulging significantly in the middle adult cohorts) Tj
T*
ET

