(   [Problem Statement]: Detail the etiology, molecular pathophysiology, and transmission mechanics of Water-Borne Bacterial Diseases: Cholera and Typhoid Fever.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Etiology, Molecular Pathophysiology, and Transmission of Water-Borne Cholera and Typhoid:) Tj
T*
(   1. Cholera \(Vibrio cholerae\):) Tj
T*
(   - Microbial Etiology: Gram-negative, comma-shaped motile bacterium with a single polar) Tj
T*
(   flagellum. Major pandemic serogroups: O1 \(classical and El Tor biotypes\) and O139.) Tj
T*
(   - Transmission Dynamics: Fecal-oral route through ingestion of drinking water or food) Tj
T*
(   cross-contaminated with human feces from infected patients or asymptomatic carriers.) Tj
T*
(   - Molecular Pathophysiology \(The Cholera Toxin Mechanism\):) Tj
T*
(   * In the small intestine, V. cholerae attaches to enterocyte microvilli via toxin-coregulated) Tj
T*
(   pili \(TCP\).) Tj
T*
(   * Secretes Cholera Toxin \(CT\): An AB5-type protein enterotoxin composed of an enzymatically) Tj
T*
(   active A subunit and five receptor-binding B subunits.) Tj
T*
(   * Subunit B binds specifically to Ganglioside GM1 receptors on the enterocyte apical plasma) Tj
T*
(   membrane, triggering endocytosis of subunit A.) Tj
T*
(   * Inside the cytosol, the A1 fragment catalyzes the ADP-ribosylation of the alpha-subunit of) Tj
T*
(   the heterotrimeric stimulatory G-protein \(Gs-alpha\), permanently locking it in an active,) Tj
T*
(   GTP-bound conformation!) Tj
T*
(   * Continuously activated Gs-alpha stimulates Adenylyl Cyclase, elevating intracellular cyclic) Tj
T*
(   AMP \(cAMP\) levels by orders of magnitude.) Tj
T*
(   * Surging cAMP phosphorylates and hyper-activates the Cystic Fibrosis Transmembrane Conductance) Tj
T*
(   Regulator \(CFTR\) chloride channels on the apical membrane, driving massive efflux of chloride) Tj
T*
(   ions \(Cl-\) into the intestinal lumen, while inhibiting electroneutral Na+/Cl- absorption.) Tj
T*
(   * Water follows the severe osmotic gradient: Enterocytes pump out massive volumes of water \(up) Tj
T*
(   to 1.0 Liter/hour!\), producing watery 'rice-water stools,' catastrophic hypovolemic shock,) Tj
T*
(   metabolic acidosis, and death within 12 to 24 hours if untreated.) Tj
T*
(   2. Typhoid Fever \(Enteric Fever, Salmonella enterica serovar Typhi\):) Tj
T*
(   - Microbial Etiology: Gram-negative, flagellated intracellular invasive rod-shaped bacterium) Tj
T*
(   bearing the Vi capsular polysaccharide antigen.) Tj
T*
(   - Transmission: Ingestion of water contaminated by human sewage from chronic biliary carriers) Tj
T*
(   \(e.g., 'Typhoid Mary'\).) Tj
T*
(   - Invasive Intracellular Pathophysiology:) Tj
T*
(   * Bacteria resist gastric acidity, penetrate the ileal mucosa via M-cells of Peyer's Patches,) Tj
T*
(   and are engulfed by intestinal macrophages.) Tj
T*
(   * Rather than being killed, S. Typhi survives inside macrophage phagosomes by suppressing the) Tj
T*
(   respiratory burst via Type III Secretion Systems \(T3SS\).) Tj
T*
(   * Macrophages transport bacteria via mesenteric lymph nodes into the bloodstream, establishing) Tj
T*
(   primary transient bacteremia.) Tj
T*
(   * Bacteria disseminate to the reticuloendothelial system \(liver, spleen, bone marrow, and) Tj
T*
(   gallbladder\).) Tj
T*
(   * After an incubation period of 7 to 14 days, secondary sustained bacteremia erupts, releasing) Tj
T*
(   endotoxins \(LPS\): continuous high remittent 'step-ladder' fever, bradycardia,) Tj
T*
(   hepatosplenomegaly, and 'rose spots' on the trunk.) Tj
T*
(   * Hyperplasia and subsequent ulceration of Peyer's patches in the terminal ileum can cause) Tj
T*
(   fatal intestinal perforation and internal peritonitis.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
