(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze the mathematical metrics used in environmental health epidemiology: Relative Risk \(RR\), Odds Ratio \(OR\), and Disability-Adjusted Life Years \(DALY\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Epidemiological Metrics in Environmental Health: Relative Risk, Odds Ratio, and DALY) Tj
T*
(   Formulation:) Tj
T*
(   1. Relative Risk \(Risk Ratio, RR\):) Tj
T*
(   - Definition: Used in prospective Cohort Studies to measure the strength of association between) Tj
T*
(   environmental exposure and disease occurrence.) Tj
T*
(   - Formulation \(2x2 Contingency Table\):) Tj
T*
(   * Exposed Group: a \(diseased\), b \(healthy\); Total exposed = \(a + b\).) Tj
T*
(   * Unexposed Group: c \(diseased\), d \(healthy\); Total unexposed = \(c + d\).) Tj
T*
(   * Cumulative Incidence in Exposed: I_e = a / \(a + b\).) Tj
T*
(   * Cumulative Incidence in Unexposed: I_u = c / \(c + d\).) Tj
T*
(   * Relative Risk: RR = I_e / I_u = [ a / \(a + b\) ] / [ c / \(c + d\) ].) Tj
T*
(   - Interpretation:) Tj
T*
(   * RR = 1.0: No association between exposure and disease.) Tj
T*
(   * RR > 1.0: Exposure is positively associated with disease \(elevated risk\).) Tj
T*
ET

