(   * RR < 1.0: Exposure is protective.) Tj
T*
(   2. Odds Ratio \(OR\):) Tj
T*
(   - Definition: Used in retrospective Case-Control Studies where incidence rates cannot be) Tj
T*
(   calculated directly because the total exposed population denominator is unknown.) Tj
T*
(   - Formulation:) Tj
T*
(   * Odds of Exposure among Cases \(Diseased\): Odds_cases = a / c.) Tj
T*
(   * Odds of Exposure among Controls \(Healthy\): Odds_controls = b / d.) Tj
T*
(   * Odds Ratio: OR = \(a / c\) / \(b / d\) = \(a * d\) / \(b * c\) \(The Cross-Product Ratio\).) Tj
T*
(   - Rare Disease Assumption: When a disease is rare in the general population \(< 5%\), the Odds) Tj
T*
(   Ratio mathematically approximates the Relative Risk: OR ~ RR.) Tj
T*
(   3. Disability-Adjusted Life Years \(DALY, Murray and Lopez, 1996\):) Tj
T*
(   - Formulated by the WHO and World Bank to quantify the Global Burden of Disease \(GBD\),) Tj
T*
(   measuring the gap between current health status and an ideal health situation where the entire) Tj
T*
(   population lives to an advanced age, free of disease and disability:) Tj
T*
(   DALY = YLL + YLD) Tj
T*
(   - 1. Years of Life Lost \(YLL\): Quantifies premature mortality:) Tj
T*
(   YLL = N * L) Tj
T*
(   Where N is the number of deaths attributable to the environmental factor, and L is the standard) Tj
T*
(   life expectancy remaining at the age of death.) Tj
T*
(   - 2. Years Lived with Disability \(YLD\): Quantifies morbidity:) Tj
T*
(   YLD = I * DW * D) Tj
T*
(   Where I is the number of incident cases, DW is the Disability Weight \(ranging from 0.0 for) Tj
T*
(   perfect health to 1.0 for death\), and D is the average duration of the disability in years.) Tj
T*
