(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze the principles of Environmental Justice, distinguishing Inter-Generational and Intra-Generational Equity, and the Basel Convention.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Environmental Justice Framework: Inter- and Intra-Generational Equity and Toxic Colonialism:) Tj
T*
(   1. Conceptual Definition of Environmental Justice \(EJ\):) Tj
T*
(   Born during the 1982 Warren County PCB protest in North Carolina, Environmental Justice asserts) Tj
T*
(   that no group of people-regardless of race, ethnicity, socioeconomic status, or) Tj
T*
(   nationality-should bear a disproportionate share of negative environmental consequences) Tj
T*
(   resulting from industrial, municipal, or commercial operations.) Tj
T*
(   2. The Two Foundations of Environmental Equity:) Tj
T*
(   - 1. Intra-Generational Equity \(Fairness within the Current Generation\):) Tj
T*
(   * Mandates that all members of the present living human generation have an equal moral right to) Tj
T*
(   clean air, safe water, and natural resources, and must not be subjected to discriminatory) Tj
T*
(   environmental degradation.) Tj
T*
(   * Addresses 'Environmental Racism' and socioeconomic disparity: Wealthy communities generate) Tj
T*
(   the majority of waste through high consumption, but toxic landfills, incinerators, and) Tj
T*
(   polluting factories are systematically sited in impoverished, marginalized, or minority) Tj
T*
(   neighborhoods lacking political lobbying power.) Tj
T*
(   - 2. Inter-Generational Equity \(Fairness Across Successive Generations\):) Tj
T*
(   * Conceptualized by legal scholar Edith Brown Weiss \(1989\): The human species holds the natural) Tj
T*
(   environment of our planet in partnership between all generations: past generations, the present) Tj
T*
(   generation, and future generations.) Tj
T*
(   * Three Basic Obligations of Inter-Generational Equity:) Tj
T*
(   - Conservation of Options: Each generation must conserve the natural and cultural resource base) Tj
T*
(   so that it does not restrict the options available to future generations.) Tj
T*
(   - Conservation of Quality: Each generation must maintain the quality of the planet so that it) Tj
T*
(   is passed on in no worse condition than that in which it was received.) Tj
T*
(   - Conservation of Access: Each generation must ensure equitable access to the legacy of past) Tj
T*
(   generations for all its members.) Tj
T*
(   3. The Basel Convention and Toxic Colonialism:) Tj
T*
(   - 'Toxic Colonialism': The exploitative practice where high-income industrialized nations) Tj
T*
(   export toxic, hazardous, and electronic wastes to impoverished developing nations \(e.g.,) Tj
T*
(   Africa, South Asia\) lacking hazardous containment facilities.) Tj
T*
(   - Basel Convention on the Control of Transboundary Movements of Hazardous Wastes \(1989\):) Tj
T*
(   * Strictly regulates cross-border movements of hazardous waste.) Tj
T*
(   * Basel Ban Amendment \(entered into force in 2019\): Prohibits all exports of hazardous wastes) Tj
T*
(   from OECD developed nations to non-OECD developing nations for any reason, including recycling,) Tj
T*
(   codifying international environmental justice into global law.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
