(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze the role of Non-Governmental Organizations \(NGOs\) and Public Interest Litigation \(PIL\) in Indian environmental governance, detailing landmark cases.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Environmental NGOs, Public Interest Litigation \(PIL\), and Judicial Governance in India:) Tj
T*
(   1. The Institutional Expansion of Locus Standi \(Justice P.N. Bhagwati, 1980s\):) Tj
T*
(   Under traditional Anglo-Saxon common law, only a person who suffered direct, personalized) Tj
T*
(   injury to legal rights possessed legal standing \(locus standi\) to file a petition in court.) Tj
T*
(   - The Public Interest Litigation \(PIL\) Revolution:) Tj
T*
(   The Supreme Court of India dismantled traditional locus standi for environmental and human) Tj
T*
(   rights violations:) Tj
T*
(   Any public-spirited citizen or voluntary NGO acting bona fide can petition the Supreme Court) Tj
T*
(   \(under Article 32\) or High Courts \(under Article 226\) on behalf of marginalized communities or) Tj
T*
(   the voiceless natural environment to enforce constitutional environmental duties.) Tj
T*
(   2. Renowned Indian Environmental NGOs and Think Tanks:) Tj
T*
